    {
      "claim": "StratonovichHAMSecondMoment.IntervalScalar.singleton_weight_le: P:interval-coefficients",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "Ledger:2105 and claims.yaml:11941 match IntervalScalar.lean:751-756: '1≤β', h,T>0, L≥1, k,i≥1 and 'h≤LT/i'. TeX:3437-3438,3452-3464 has β>2 and the same length substitution at λ=k/T. Lean's weaker β condition and extracted L^β agree with the stated singleton step."
    },
    {
      "claim": "StratonovichHAMSecondMoment.IntervalScalar.cross_weight_le: P:interval-coefficients",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "Ledger:2106 and claims.yaml:11948 match IntervalScalar.lean:783-787: 'alpha≤2', 'alpha0≤2', positive h,T, L≥1, k,i≥1 and h≤LT/i. TeX:3439,3453-3464 uses α<2 and p=2−α0>0. The resulting L^β T^β k^(α−2)i^−p bound is a valid generalized version of precisely that substitution."
    },
    {
      "claim": "StratonovichHAMSecondMoment.IntervalScalar.higher_weight_le: P:interval-coefficients",
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "reason": "Ledger:2107 and claims.yaml:11955 match IntervalScalar.lean:813-818: '1≤β', '2≤l', positive h,T, L≥1, k,i≥1 and h≤LT/i. TeX:3448-3456 uses β>2,l≥2 to make all powers positive. Lean allows nonnegative limiting exponents and proves the same whole-weight estimate; it does not incorrectly bound the inverse-length factor separately."
